WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.300 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  what's your ambition take a second pause  and think about it  what's an ambition that you have for  yourself whether it's to become the next  blockchain investor to launch that  startup you've always dreamed of or to  become the next Secretary of Defense  whatever it might be imagine how amazing  you're going to feel when you realize  that ambition now let's just say I  arbitrarily decide to draw a line down  the room I'll include Geordi in the line  on this side I tell all of you on this  side of the room but actually just  because I decided you don't get to  realize your ambition that because you  sat in that seat you don't get the  opportunity to pursue your dreams  imagine how much it would suck to have  your ambition taken away from you this  is what happens to women all over the  world let me walk you across the world  for a second to a rural part of Kenya  it's a scorching hot day and I'm sitting  under a shady tree talking to a bunch of  girls and asking them that same question  that I just asked all of you what's your  ambition the girls respond to be a  doctor to be a lawyer to be an astronaut  just like you and me these girls  imagine a bright future for themselves  yet as my conversation with them  continues I begin to realize that  they're not going to realize their  ambition that because of factors beyond  their control they won't have the  opportunity to pursue their dreams one  of the girls Dorothy who I got to know  well during this time tells me that  every morning she wakes up at 4:00 a.m.  before school and she walks two hours to  a well gets water and carries it home  for her family after school she's the  one who's expected to take care of her  younger siblings and  cook dinner for the family while  Dorothy's brothers are studying her and  millions of girls like her around the  world are expected to take on household  work when money is tight parents send a  clear signal by sending their sons to  school but asking their daughters to  stay home and when girls do make it to  the classroom they have to listen to  their teachers make comments like don't  do math like a girl girls are constantly  told implicitly and explicitly that  their ambitions are not as important as  their brothers and what does this mean  this means that less than 15 percent of  the girls that I met that summer will  graduate from high school their  ambitions of being doctors lawyers and  astronauts will get crushed and instead  they'll get married young give birth to  multiple children and then they'll ask  their daughters to stay home and help  with household tasks their daughters  will become the next generation of women  who have to carry the water this is the  heartbreaking reality of so many of the  girls who I met in mohuru Bay Kenya  seven years ago at that time I was  thinking what some of you might be  thinking right now this is extremely sad  but it's also kind of expected in a  low-income and rural place like mohuru  Bay I left a situation feeling totally  helpless I wanted to make a difference  but I didn't know how several years  later I decided to apply to Stanford its  Graduate School of Business  when I got the infamous call from  Derrick Bolton I was elated I was so  excited  and I thought that at such a  well-resourced and elite place like the  GSB I would find my answers to these  questions I also assumed that for the  first time in my life I would be in an  environment where women's ambition was  not only encouraged it was expected  however I haven't always found that to  be the case let me take you to an uber  ride couple months ago with a couple of  classmates maybe some of you in the room  we're riding back home after a late  night of BPL so another night at the  patio there's a female classmate and too  close guy friends who are in the car and  we started joking around about being  single at the GSB some of you will also  be able to relate to that we were  talking about how rough it can be and  those questions that constantly plague  us should I bring a notebook on my  ambigú date at our buckle tomorrow can  one of you please create an excel  tracker so I can know who's still single  but then all of a sudden the  conversation turns to something I didn't  expect my female classmate turned to me  and said Sadhna I don't think I'm gonna  keep working once I get married I doubt  I'll find a husband who will support my  career ambition I was shocked  this is Stanford's Graduate School of  Business  she went on to explain that because  women are more nurturing it only makes  sense that she's the one who's expected  to take care of her kids and it turns  out that my friends experience is not an  anomaly a recent Harvard Business Review  study shows that single MBA women  continue to downplay their ambition they  continue to feel like there is a  trade-off between ambition and marriage  on the first day of an MBA program these  students were asked to state their  career preferences when they were told  that their responses would be shared  with their male classmates they said  that they wanted to make $20,000 less  they also said that they're less able  and willing to lead  shouldn't this concern everybody in the  room we want women to be smart loving  nurturing but never ambitious to be  honest with you I even hesitated to give  a talk on female ambition because I  thought one of you in the room would  hear it and think that I was too  ambitious and then I realized this is  the problem research also shows that  women in the u.s. spend 25 hours per  week on household tasks women that are  working and their male counterparts in  the same professional tier spend only 10  hours per week on household tasks so  what shows up in the homes and  classrooms in Kenya actually shows up  right here at the GSB even in the United  States women carry the water no matter  where we are and no matter how rich how  poor there is a common theme all across  the development curve we fail to support  women's ambition it's easy to hear this  talk and to not realize the personal  implications of it to be honest I didn't  realize it until it happened to me I  recently found myself in a situation  where I was told that there was no room  for my ambition I was in a three-year  relationship with a guy who I loved and  wanted to marry yet time and time again  in the relationship there was so many  subtle and some not so subtle signs that  my ambition was a problem my partner's  fear but because I wanted to have a  career I wouldn't make a good mother or  thoughts in my own head that if I just  turned down my consulting offer after  business school maybe I could make him  love me more but then in that moment I  suddenly thought back to Dorothy  and all of the girls who had met in  Kenya I thought of my 9 year old niece  who loves Bill and I have a science guy  and wants to be a scientist when she  grows up with their faces in my mind I  finally gathered the courage to leave  the relationship rather than to leave my  ambition behind in writing this talk I  finally have realized the answer to my  question I finally know what we can do  to help those girls in Kenya and it  starts with making a change right here  at the GSB if you're a woman don't let  someone else's expectations dictate your  level of ambition don't downplay your  dreams for somebody else and if you're a  man you play a huge role in all this you  have an amazing opportunity to help  women realize their ambition don't be  afraid of ambitious women we don't bite  I promise date ambitious women marry  them celebrate their accomplishments in  your future workplace encourage women to  be ambitious and support their careers  and more tactically when you're in a  domestic partnership make sure you're  taking on your share of childcare and  household work only then we all work  together and balance the load can we  make sure that women aren't the only  ones carrying the water thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

